= Chemical equilibrium — Simple =

Some chemical reactions can go forward and backward. At '''chemical equilibrium''', both directions keep happening, but at equal rates, so the overall amounts of substances stay steady.

This does not mean the molecules have stopped reacting.

Changing temperature or the amounts of ingredients can shift the balance. A catalyst may help the system reach equilibrium faster, but it does not change the final balance at the same temperature.

Acid–base equilibria help explain a solution's [[pH]].

